window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-27"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "This is one sequence cut into pieces from a fixed set, with no reordering, so I want to fix the indexing convention before I write a line: everything here is indexed by prefix length, not by character position.", "questions": ["Can a vocabulary entry appear more than once in a single segmentation?", "When several segmentations are legal and the caller wants only one, which should I return?", "Is the vocabulary fixed for the lifetime of the segmenter, so I can precompute from it in init?"], "assumptions": ["Entries may repeat within one segmentation, and the identifier must be covered completely with no gaps.", "The canonical single answer is the one whose last segment is shortest, applied again at every earlier cut.", "The vocabulary is fixed at construction, so the lookup and the longest entry length are computed once in init.", "An empty vocabulary, an empty segment text, a negative penalty and a repeated segment text are construction failures; an identifier that does not segment is a typed failure at the call."], "edgeCases": ["the empty identifier, which is covered by no segments at all", "an identifier longer than every vocabulary entry", "a run of repeated characters, where greedy matching fails and the enumeration count explodes", "an identifier with one unknown character in the middle", "a vocabulary where a long entry overlaps several short ones, so the cheapest split has more segments than the shortest"], "restate": "Given a vocabulary of known segments with penalties, decide whether an unspaced identifier splits cleanly, return one canonical split, enumerate every split, and return the least-penalty split."}, "solution": {"pitch": "One array indexed by prefix length, and an inner loop over where the last piece began. That single shape answers three of the four parts: yes-or-no, cheapest, and with one recorded cut, which split. The third part is the one that cannot use it, and saying why is most of the value.", "dataStructures": ["A dictionary from segment text to penalty, which serves as both the membership test and the cost lookup so there is only one thing to keep in step.", "The longest entry length, cached in init, to bound the inner loop so it never proposes a piece nothing could match.", "The identifier as an array of characters, converted once, because stepping a string index through a nested loop is linear work per step."], "types": ["VocabularySegment pairs the text with its penalty, so a vocabulary is one list rather than a list and a parallel map.", "Segmentation returns the pieces and the penalty together, since a caller comparing splits needs both.", "SegmenterError separates the four construction failures from the one call-time failure, so a caller can tell a bad vocabulary from an identifier this vocabulary simply does not cover."], "composition": "canSegment and firstSegmentation are one private scan that returns both the coverage flags and the recorded cuts; the first reads the last flag, the second walks the cuts. cheapestSegmentation reuses that same loop with a minimum and hands its own cuts to the very same walk-back helper. allSegmentations calls canSegment as a cheap refusal and then deliberately abandons the table.", "tradeoff": "A trie over the vocabulary would remove every substring allocation from the inner loop and bound it by the longest entry without a separate length cache. For interview-scale identifiers a dictionary plus that cached bound is the same complexity with far less code, and I would name the trie as the move if the identifiers were long or the vocabulary huge.", "trap": "Trying to keep tabulating in the enumeration part. A table entry holding a growing list of splits copies those lists at every step and buys nothing, because the output can be exponentially large no matter how it is stored. The memo bounds the work per start position; it cannot bound the answer."}, "coding": {"order": ["Validate the vocabulary in init and cache the penalty lookup and the longest entry length there.", "Write the private scan that fills the coverage flags and the recorded cuts, and let Part 1 read its last flag.", "Write the walk-back helper, then Part 2 is two lines on top of the scan.", "Write the memoised recursion for the enumeration, stating out loud why the table is being left behind.", "Finish with the cheapest split by copying the scan's loop and replacing the acceptance with a minimum."], "commentary": ["Everything here is indexed by prefix length, so entry zero is the empty prefix, and I am fixing that convention now rather than half way through.", "The empty prefix is covered because there is nothing left to cover, and that base case is what every other entry is eventually built on.", "I scan the split points nearest first and stop at the first that works, which is what makes the canonical answer the one with the shortest last segment.", "This part cannot use the table, because the answer is a set of splits and not a number. I keep the memo and change the shape.", "The memo means each start position is solved once. It does not mean the call is fast on a pathological run, because the output itself can be exponential.", "The cheapest split is the same loop with a minimum, and it reuses the walk-back from Part 2 so the two parts cannot drift apart on ties."], "checkIn": "Before Part 3, say: this is the part where the table stops being the right tool. Do you want me to explain why before I write the recursion, or write it and then justify the change of shape?"}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Walk the coverage flags over the worked identifier: prefix four is covered by the first entry, prefix nine by the second and also by the long entry, and prefix sixteen by the last, so the identifier segments.", "Walk the recorded cuts back from sixteen: the cut at sixteen points to nine, the cut at nine points to four, and four points to zero, giving three segments in reading order.", "Walk the enumeration from position zero: the four-character piece leads to one split of the rest, the nine-character piece leads to another, so exactly two splits come back and the shorter first piece comes first.", "Walk the penalties on the same identifier: the two-segment split costs five plus one and the three-segment split costs one plus one plus one, so the cheapest answer has more segments than the shortest one, which is the whole point of that fixture."], "tests": "Run swift test --filter Problem27LogTokenSegmenterTests and read the four suites in part order. The run of repeated characters in the first suite is what catches greedy matching, and the twenty-character run in the third is what catches a missing memo.", "complexity": "The prefix scans are O(n times the longest entry length) comparisons, with a substring built per candidate piece, and O(n) space. The enumeration is bounded per start position by the memo but its output can be exponential in the identifier length, so its total cost is the number of splits it has to emit and not the number of states it visits.", "moreTime": "Allowing unmatched characters at a fixed cost turns the feasibility question into a minimisation that is always defined, which is the same loop with one extra branch. Counting the splits rather than listing them goes back to the table and needs only a sum where the flag was, and a minimum-cuts variant over pieces validated by a precomputed property is the same prefix loop sitting on top of a small second table."}, "code": "/// One known segment in the service registry's vocabulary, with the confidence\n/// penalty the pipeline pays for using it. Lower is more confident.\npublic struct VocabularySegment: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let text: String\n    public let penalty: Int\n\n    public init(text: String, penalty: Int) {\n        self.text = text\n        self.penalty = penalty\n    }\n}\n\n/// A segmentation and what it costs.\npublic struct Segmentation: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let segments: [String]\n    public let totalPenalty: Int\n\n    public init(segments: [String], totalPenalty: Int) {\n        self.segments = segments\n        self.totalPenalty = totalPenalty\n    }\n}\n\npublic enum SegmenterError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case emptyVocabulary\n    case emptySegmentText\n    case negativePenalty(String)\n    case duplicateSegmentText(String)\n    case noValidSegmentation\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\npublic struct LogTokenSegmenter: Sendable {\n    /// The vocabulary, arranged for the two questions the scan actually asks:\n    /// is this piece known, and what does it cost. The longest entry bounds the\n    /// inner loop, so a scan never proposes a piece no entry could match.\n    private let penalties: [String: Int]\n    private let longestSegmentLength: Int\n\n    public init(vocabulary: [VocabularySegment]) throws(SegmenterError) {\n        guard !vocabulary.isEmpty else { throw .emptyVocabulary }\n\n        var penalties: [String: Int] = [:]\n        for segment in vocabulary {\n            guard !segment.text.isEmpty else { throw .emptySegmentText }\n            guard segment.penalty >= 0 else { throw .negativePenalty(segment.text) }\n            guard penalties[segment.text] == nil else { throw .duplicateSegmentText(segment.text) }\n            penalties[segment.text] = segment.penalty\n        }\n\n        self.penalties = penalties\n        self.longestSegmentLength = vocabulary.map(\\.text.count).max() ?? 0\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - Can the identifier be segmented?\n\n    /// Whether the identifier is entirely covered by known segments.\n    ///\n    /// One entry means \"the first i characters split cleanly\", so the empty\n    /// prefix is the base case and it is true: nothing left to cover is covered.\n    public func canSegment(_ identifier: String) -> Bool {\n        let characters = Array(identifier)\n        return reachability(characters).feasible[characters.count]\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - Return one canonical segmentation\n\n    /// One segmentation, chosen canonically: at every cut the shortest possible\n    /// last segment wins, because the split points are scanned from the closest\n    /// one backwards and the first that works is recorded.\n    public func firstSegmentation(_ identifier: String) throws(SegmenterError) -> [String] {\n        let characters = Array(identifier)\n        let table = reachability(characters)\n        guard table.feasible[characters.count] else { throw .noValidSegmentation }\n        return rebuild(from: table.previousCut, characters: characters)\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - Enumerate every segmentation\n\n    /// Every segmentation of the identifier, ordered by ascending first-segment\n    /// length, and recursively so within each branch.\n    ///\n    /// Part 1's table answers \"is there one\". It cannot answer \"give me all of\n    /// them\", because the answer is not a number and no single entry can hold a\n    /// growing list of splits cheaply. So the shape changes from a table walked\n    /// forwards to a recursion over start positions, and the memo is what keeps\n    /// the work per position bounded. The memo bounds the work, not the size of\n    /// the output: an identifier can genuinely have exponentially many splits.\n    public func allSegmentations(_ identifier: String) -> [[String]] {\n        let characters = Array(identifier)\n        guard canSegment(identifier) else { return [] }\n\n        var memo: [Int: [[String]]] = [:]\n\n        func segmentations(from start: Int) -> [[String]] {\n            if let cached = memo[start] { return cached }\n            if start == characters.count { return [[]] }\n\n            var found: [[String]] = []\n            let furthest = min(characters.count, start + longestSegmentLength)\n            for end in stride(from: start + 1, through: furthest, by: 1) {\n                let piece = String(characters[start..<end])\n                guard penalties[piece] != nil else { continue }\n                for rest in segmentations(from: end) {\n                    found.append([piece] + rest)\n                }\n            }\n\n            memo[start] = found\n            return found\n        }\n\n        return segmentations(from: 0)\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 4 - Cheapest segmentation\n\n    /// The least-penalty segmentation. Fewer segments is not automatically\n    /// cheaper, which is why this cannot be read off Part 2's answer.\n    ///\n    /// This is Part 1's loop with a minimum in place of the disjunction, and it\n    /// hands its cut record to the same rebuild helper Part 2 uses. Ties resolve\n    /// to the shorter last segment, matching Part 2's canonical order, because\n    /// the strict comparison keeps the first cut a backwards scan reaches.\n    public func cheapestSegmentation(_ identifier: String) throws(SegmenterError) -> Segmentation {\n        let characters = Array(identifier)\n        let unreachable = Int.max\n\n        var cost = Array(repeating: unreachable, count: characters.count + 1)\n        var previousCut = Array(repeating: Int?.none, count: characters.count + 1)\n        cost[0] = 0\n\n        for end in stride(from: 1, through: characters.count, by: 1) {\n            for start in cuts(before: end) where cost[start] != unreachable {\n                guard let penalty = penalties[String(characters[start..<end])] else { continue }\n                if cost[start] + penalty < cost[end] {\n                    cost[end] = cost[start] + penalty\n                    previousCut[end] = start\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        guard cost[characters.count] != unreachable else { throw .noValidSegmentation }\n        return Segmentation(\n            segments: rebuild(from: previousCut, characters: characters),\n            totalPenalty: cost[characters.count]\n        )\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Shared machinery\n\n    /// The split points that could begin the last segment ending at `end`,\n    /// nearest first. Nearest first is what makes the canonical segmentation\n    /// prefer the shorter last segment, and bounding the scan by the longest\n    /// vocabulary entry keeps it from proposing pieces nothing could match.\n    private func cuts(before end: Int) -> StrideThrough<Int> {\n        stride(from: end - 1, through: max(0, end - longestSegmentLength), by: -1)\n    }\n\n    /// Whether each prefix splits cleanly, and where the last segment of that\n    /// prefix began. Part 1 reads the final flag; Part 2 walks the cuts back.\n    private func reachability(_ characters: [Character]) -> (feasible: [Bool], previousCut: [Int?]) {\n        var feasible = Array(repeating: false, count: characters.count + 1)\n        var previousCut = Array(repeating: Int?.none, count: characters.count + 1)\n        feasible[0] = true\n\n        for end in stride(from: 1, through: characters.count, by: 1) {\n            for start in cuts(before: end) where feasible[start] {\n                if penalties[String(characters[start..<end])] != nil {\n                    feasible[end] = true\n                    previousCut[end] = start\n                    break\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        return (feasible, previousCut)\n    }\n\n    /// Walks a cut record back from the whole identifier to the empty prefix and\n    /// turns it into segments in reading order.\n    private func rebuild(from previousCut: [Int?], characters: [Character]) -> [String] {\n        var reversedSegments: [String] = []\n        var end = characters.count\n        while end > 0, let start = previousCut[end] {\n            reversedSegments.append(String(characters[start..<end]))\n            end = start\n        }\n        return reversedSegments.reversed()\n    }\n}\n"};
